e also skip if blocked? Actually, the description: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So the block is for ancestors to prevent future selection. So we should check both: if the current cluster is itself blocked (by a previous selection of a descendant), then skip. And also check if any descendant is already selected (which would have blocked its ancestors, so current cluster would be blocked). So we can maintain a \"blocked\" set. Alternatively, we can maintain a \"selected\" set and during iteration check if any descendant of current is selected. That's O(n^2) but fine.\n\nSince we have to block ancestors after selection, we can simply after selecting, add all ancestors of selected to a set \"blocked\". Then when iterating, skip if cluster in blocked or if any descendant in selected? But if we block ancestors, that covers the descendant check? Not exactly: If we have selected cluster A, then its ancestors are blocked. Later we consider cluster B which is a sibling of A. B is not an ancestor of A, but A is not a descendant of B either (unless A is in B's subtree). So descendant check is separate. Actually, we need both: For current cluster C, if any selected cluster is a descendant of C, then C cannot be selected. That is equivalent to: C is an ancestor of a selected cluster. So C should be blocked. So after selecting a cluster, we should block all its ancestors. Then later when we encounter any of those ancestors, they will be blocked. So we don't need to check descendants again if we have blocked ancestors. However, we also need to consider the case where a cluster has both selected descendants and selected ancestors? But if a descendant is selected, its ancestors are blocked. So that cluster would already be blocked. So if we maintain a blocked set that includes all ancestors of selected clusters, then for any new cluster C, we just check if C is in blocked. Because if any descendant of C is selected