s even more stable? That descendant would be considered earlier (since higher stability). So the ancestor would be blocked by that descendant. So in practice, the algorithm ensures that no cluster is selected if any descendant is already selected. That means after selecting a cluster, we block its ancestors to prevent them from being selected later, but we do not block its descendants. So it's possible to select a descendant after its ancestor? Only if the ancestor was not selected yet. The order ensures that if the ancestor is more stable, it will be considered before the descendant (assuming stability descending). But if the ancestor is selected, then its descendants are not blocked, but later when a descendant is considered, it would check if any descendant of it is selected (it might have no descendants selected), and then it would try to select it, but then it would block its ancestors which includes the already selected ancestor. That would be invalid. Therefore, to maintain consistency, the algorithm must also check that no ancestor is already selected before selecting a cluster. The description says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". It does not mention ancestor. But the typical EOM selection does check both: you cannot select a cluster if any ancestor or descendant is already selected. However, the description explicitly says \"then block its ancestors\" which implies that after selection, ancestors become blocked. So if an ancestor is already selected, and we try to select a descendant, the ancestor would be blocked? That would be ambiguous.\n\nLet's look at standard HDBSCAN implementation. In the paper, the excess of mass selection: Starting from leaves, compute stability. Then propagate up: if sum of children stabilities > parent stability, set parent stability to sum of children and mark children as selected; else, keep parent stability and mark parent as selected. That's a different algorithm. Here we have a top-down ranking approach. The description matches the \"eom\" selection from the HDBSCAN Python library? In the scikit-learn-contrib/hdbscan code, they have a function `_condensed_tree._select_clusters` that does: sort clusters by decreasing stability, then for each cluster, if no descendants selected, select it and block ancestors. That's exactly what is described. And they do not check ancestors selected. So if a more stable ancestor is selected first, then later a descendant is considered, it would have no selected descendant (the ancestor is not a descendant), so it would be selected, and then it would block the already selected ancestor. But that would cause both to be selected? Actually, after selecting the descendant, we block its ancestors. If the ancestor is already selected, we would be trying to block it but it's already selected. That would be invalid, and the result would have both selected, which is not allowed. But does the algorithm allow that? Let's think: If the ancestor has higher stability and is considered first, it gets selected. Its descendants are not blocked. Then later when we consider a descendant, it sees no selected descendant (since none), so it selects it. Then it blocks its ancestors, which includes the already selected ancestor. Now we have both selected, violating the condition that no ancestor-descendant pair should be selected. But the algorithm as described in HDBSCAN's code actually checks for \"selected descendants\" only, and after selecting, it blocks ancestors. However, they also have a step where they initially mark all clusters as not selected, and then process in order, and they skip clusters that are already in a \"blocked\" set (which includes ancestors of previously selected). So if an ancestor is selected, its descendants are not blocked initially. But later when a descendant is considered, it will not be blocked (since not in blocked set), and it will have no selected descendants (none), so it will be selected. Then it will block its ancestors. But then they would end up with both selected? I recall that in the actual implementation, they also check that the cluster itself is not in the blocked set (i.e., it hasn't been blocked by a descendant). That would be the case if an ancestor was selected first, the ancestor is not blocked, but when selecting the descendant, the ancestor becomes blocked. But the ancestor is already selected. That seems inconsistent.\n\nLet's look at the HDBSCAN source code (from GitHub): In `hdbscan/_hdbscan_tree.pyx`, function `_select_clusters` (or similar). I recall that they do: sort by decreasing stability, then iterate. For each cluster, if it is not already in `blocked` set, and if none of its descendants are in selected set, then select it, and add all its ancestors to blocked set. That ensures that a descendant cannot be selected after an ancestor because the descendant will have the ancestor in its descendant list? No, descendant has ancestor in its ancestry, not descendant. The check is only for descendants in selected set. If ancestor is selected, it is not a descendant of descendant. So descendant would pass the check. Then when descendant blocks ancestors, the ancestor becomes blocked, but it remains selected. That yields both selected. However, is that correct? In the HDBSCAN algorithm, they perform a bottom-up propagation, not this greedy selection. The greedy selection described is for the \"excess of mass\" variant? I'm not sure.\n\nGiven the problem statement explicitly says: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So we follow that literally. So we only check for descendants. After selection, we block ancestors. We do not check for ancestors already selected. This could lead to both an ancestor and descendant being selected if the ancestor has higher stability and is selected first. But is that possible? Suppose ancestor stability 10, descendant stability 9. Sorted order: ancestor first (higher stability). It has no selected descendant, so selected. Then block its ancestors (none). Next descendant: no selected descendant (none), so selected, then block its ancestors (which includes the ancestor). Now both are selected. But the ancestor is already selected, but we are blocking it now, but it's already selected. The blocked set is used to skip future clusters, but ancestor is already selected. The result would have both, which might be allowed? In HDBSCAN, the flat clustering should have no hierarchical relationship among selected clusters. So this would be invalid. However, maybe the problem expects that we do not check ancestors, and that the blocking after selection ensures that if a descendant is selected after an ancestor, the ancestor is blocked but remains selected? That would be a contradiction. But perhaps the problem expects that the descendant's blocking prevents the ancestor from being selected in the future, but since it's already selected, it's fine? But then we have a violation.\n\nLet's read the description again: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This implies that when considering a cluster, we check if any already selected cluster is a descendant of this cluster. If there is, then we skip (do not select). If not, we select it, and then we block its ancestors (so that they cannot be selected later). This ensures that once a cluster is selected, its ancestors become ineligible for selection. But what about ancestors that are already selected? That case would occur only if the ancestor was selected before checking the descendant. That is possible if the ancestor appears earlier in the sorted order (higher stability). In that case, when we later consider the descendant, we check for selected descendants: none, so we select it. Then we block its ancestors, which include the already selected ancestor. But that ancestor is already selected; we cannot \"unselect\" it. So