e_select` or `_compute_stability`. Actually, I think the standard is different: they compute stability for each cluster, then do a bottom-up traversal to decide whether to keep a cluster or its children. The greedy top-down approach described might be a simplified version. Given the problem is from a coding challenge, likely they expect the straightforward implementation: sort by stability descending, then iterate, selecting if no descendant already selected, and then blocking ancestors. They might not worry about the double selection because stability ordering ensures that descendants have lower stability, so they would come later, and when they are considered, they would see that they have no selected descendant, so they get selected, but then block ancestors. The result would be that multiple clusters on the same branch could be selected. But is that allowed? The problem says \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\". So if we have a chain of clusters A -> B -> C (A parent of B, B parent of C), suppose stability A=10, B=9, C=8. Sorted: A, B, C. A selected first. Then B: no selected descendant (C not selected yet), so B selected, block ancestors (A). Now A is blocked but already selected. Then C: no selected descendant, so C selected, block ancestors (B and A). Now all three selected. That seems wrong. But the block set is used to skip future clusters? Not here, because C is not in blocked (still not blocked). So all three selected. That would give overlapping clusters. Probably not what is intended.\n\nI suspect the intended algorithm is the one from HDBSCAN where you do a bottom-up selection using the sum of children stabilities. But the problem statement explicitly says \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID. Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This is a common pattern in competitive programming problems: sort by descending stability, then greedily select if no descendant already selected, and then mark ancestors as unavailable. This ensures that you never select a cluster if any of its descendants is already selected. Since descendants are less stable (because sorted descending), they come later. So the first cluster considered (highest stability) has no descendants selected (since none selected yet), so it is selected. Then we block its ancestors. But we also need to ensure that when we later consider its descendants, they are blocked because their ancestors are blocked? Actually, descendants are not ancestors. They are children. The blocking is only for ancestors. So descendants of a selected cluster are not blocked. So they will be considered later and may be selected. But they have a selected descendant? The selected cluster is an ancestor, not a descendant. The check is for selected descendants. So when considering a child cluster, does it have any selected descendant? Its descendant is the grandchild, not the parent. So it would have no selected descendant, and thus would be selected. Then it blocks its ancestors (including the already selected parent). So now the parent is blocked, but it's already selected. This leads to multiple selections in the same lineage. To prevent that, we also need to check that no ancestor is already selected. But the problem doesn't say that.\n\nMaybe the \"then block its ancestors\" is meant to prevent future selection of ancestors, but it doesn't prevent selection of descendants because descendants are not ancestors. So the algorithm as described would allow multiple nested selections. But then the final set will contain multiple clusters in the same branch, which is not a valid flat clustering. So likely the problem expects that we also check that the cluster is not already blocked by a descendant (i.e., its ancestors are not selected). Actually, if a descendant is selected, its ancestors get blocked. So if we later consider an ancestor, it will be blocked. That prevents an ancestor from being selected after a descendant. But if an ancestor is selected first, its descendants are not blocked. To prevent descendants from being selected, we need to have the condition that we only select a cluster if no descendant is already selected. That is exactly the condition, but if the ancestor is selected first, then later when we consider the descendant, does the descendant have a selected descendant? No, the ancestor is not a descendant. So the descendant passes the condition. So to prevent the descendant from being selected, we need to also check that the descendant's ancestors (i.e., the selected ancestor) are not selected. The problem does not mention that.\n\nGiven this confusion, we need to infer the correct behavior from the fixture examples. But the fixture only provides three identical cases with same cluster structure. The expected output is not given. We might need to reason about what makes sense for a \"condensed tree stability\" task. Typically, the selection method is \"excess of mass\" which selects clusters based on stability and ensures that selected clusters are not nested. The greedy algorithm that selects clusters in order of decreasing stability and then blocks ancestors does achieve no nesting if you also block descendants? Actually, by blocking ancestors after selection, you ensure that no future selection can include an ancestor. But you don't block descendants. So descendants can still be selected. To prevent that, you need to also block descendants? Or you need to iterate bottom-up? The standard algorithm from hdbscan's `_select_clusters` does exactly what I described: sort descending stability, then for each cluster, if it's not in blocked set and none of its descendants are selected, then select it and add all its ancestors to blocked set. This works because when a descendant is selected later, its ancestors are blocked, but the descendant itself is not blocked. However, if an ancestor is selected first, its descendants a